;; spec:   syntax §3.16 (reflection at expansion time)
;; expect: accept
;; result: 12
;; audit:  clean
;; User macros calling enum-variants and enum? on the built-in Option:
;;   syntax §3.16: "The same reflection exists for enums (Decided), for
;;   an enum Name defined earlier in the module or imported, and for the
;;   built-in Option and Form: (enum? 'Name) | bool ... (enum-variants
;;   'Name) | (Vec Form): one (List [(Sym "Variant") type*]) per variant"
;; and the types (crates/fibref/src/types/builtins.rs agrees):
;;   enum-variants : (fn (Form) (Vec Form)), enum? : (fn (Form) bool).
;; Option has two variants and is an enum: 2 + 10 = 12.
;;
;; Observed (fibref run):
;;   rejected:
;;   012-enum-reflection-returns-a-form.fib:30:26: macro nvariants failed:
;;   lib/prelude.fib:91:3: trap: no match clause matched
;; (vec-count's match on its (Vec a) argument finds neither VecEmpty nor
;; VecOf). With only the enum? macro in main:
;;   macro isenum failed: ...: internal error: not a bool: Obj(ObjId(2))
;; Diagnosis: eval (crates/fibref/src/eval/forms.rs, expansion_builtin
;; returns form_value of the reflected Form: a Form object, (Vec [..]) or
;; (Bool true), where the type promises a (Vec Form) or a bool). Checked
;; code then reads a value of one type as another; in the interpreter
;; that is a failed match, in compiled code it would read a Form object
;; through the Vec layout. enum-params fails the same way.
(defmacro nvariants (name) (Int (count (enum-variants name)) :i64))
(defmacro isenum (name) (if (enum? name) (quote 10) (quote 0)))

(defun main () -> i64 (+ (nvariants Option) (isenum Option)))
